{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Stopped after genuine plan-review FAIL following one findings-driven description revision. Latest blocking finding verbatim: The plan omits the gate-workflow file that dispatches overlay reviewers: src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/code-review.yaml lists every overlay explicitly in BOTH its round_a (lines 105-119) and round_b (lines 145-159) criteria blocks as `{ prompt: code-review-<id>, when: \"${{ steps.*.outputs.include_<id> }}\" }`. The plan's edit set (registry.py, criteria_routing.json, code-review-base.md, two new files) plus the claim that activation is 'wired through the same registry mechanisms existing overlays use' does not name this file. The registry auto-derives the `include_failover` boolean (registry.py:636), but the reviewer-dispatch lines are hand-maintained, so without adding `code-review-failover` to both criteria lists the overlay is registered and triggerable yet never invoked by a reviewer. Suggested fix: add `code-review-failover` to round_a/round_b criteria in `src/rebar/llm/workflow/gates/code-review.yaml`."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788806008715839001,"uuid":"05216c77-7ea6-462f-8a58-edde4b9fa0bd"}